---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import InfoSection from '../components/InfoSection.astro';
import SpecTable from '../components/SpecTable.astro';
---

<InfoLayout
  path="/box-styles/"
  title="Box Styles | Tuck End, Auto-Bottom, Rigid"
  description="Carton closures, base styles, sleeves, trays and rigid constructions for lip packaging, and which pack each one suits."
  h1="Box Styles and Structures"
  eyebrow="Specification"
  lead="Structure is a packing and merchandising decision more than a visual one. The closure affects how fast your line runs, and the base decides whether a full carton stays shut when it is lifted."
  ogTitle="Carton Structures for Lip Product Packaging"
  ogDescription="Tuck ends, seal ends, auto-bottoms, sleeves, trays, hanging tabs and rigid constructions compared."
  points={['Closures and bases', 'Windows, tabs and inserts', 'Rigid constructions']}
  ctaHeading="Tell us how the carton gets packed"
  ctaBody="Whether you fill by hand or on an automated line changes which closure we recommend. Send that detail with your dimensions and we will propose a structure with the reasoning."
>
  <InfoSection
    id="closures"
    heading="Closures"
    intro="How the carton opens and shuts. On a hand-filled product this is the choice that most affects how long packing takes."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Carton closure styles."
      columns={['Closure', 'How it works', 'Suits', 'Notes']}
      rows={[
        [
          'Straight tuck end',
          'Both flaps tuck from the same side',
          'Shelf display and boxed e-commerce',
          'Front panel stays uninterrupted for artwork',
        ],
        [
          'Reverse tuck end',
          'Flaps tuck from opposite ends',
          'Hand-packing lines',
          'Same cost as straight tuck; some fillers prefer it',
        ],
        [
          'Seal end',
          'Glued closed after filling',
          'Automated co-packing',
          'Needs a sealer; strongest closure',
        ],
        [
          'Tuck top with dust flaps',
          'Tuck flap plus side flaps',
          'Slightly heavier contents',
          'More material, better contents retention',
        ],
        [
          'Sleeve over tray',
          'A printed band slides over an inner tray',
          'Higher price points',
          'Two components, more presence, more cost',
        ],
        [
          'Hinged lid',
          'Lid folds back from one edge',
          'Sets and multipacks',
          'Reads more considered than a tuck flap',
        ],
      ]}
    />
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="bases"
    heading="Bases, and why they matter more than the top"
    intro="The base is the part that fails. A carton that opens downward when someone picks it up loses the product on the floor."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Base styles and the weight each handles."
      columns={['Base', 'Assembly', 'Holds', 'Use when']}
      rows={[
        ['Tuck flap base', 'Tucked by hand', 'Very light contents', 'A single lip balm stick or gloss tube'],
        ['Snap-lock base', 'Locks flat as the carton is opened', 'Moderate weight', 'Two items, or a tall carton'],
        ['Auto-bottom', 'Pre-glued, forms as the carton opens', 'Heaviest folding-carton loads', 'Multipacks, three-packs, glass primaries'],
        ['Seal end base', 'Glued shut on the line', 'High', 'Automated filling operations'],
        ['Rigid base', 'Assembled from greyboard', 'Highest', 'Gift and presentation boxes'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="note">
      Auto-bottom cartons cost more per unit than a tuck base but assemble faster,
      which on a hand-packing operation frequently pays for itself in labour. Ask
      for both prices if you are packing thousands of units by hand.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="windows-tabs"
    heading="Windows and hanging tabs"
    intro="Both remove material from a panel, and both therefore interact with board weight."
  >
    <p class="measure">
      A window lets the product sell itself, which matters most where shoppers are
      matching a colour. It can be left open or covered with a clear film patch,
      and on any pack that hangs the patch is worth having: it keeps dust out and
      restores a surprising amount of stiffness to a panel that has just had a
      large hole cut in it.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      A hanging tab is die-cut from the same board as the carton, so there is no
      separate plastic hook to source or dispose of. Euro slot, round hole, delta
      cut and extended header are the common shapes, and the retailer usually
      dictates which. Where a window sits directly below a tab, the load path is
      interrupted and reinforcement becomes necessary — set out in detail on the
      <a href="/hang-tab-lipstick-boxes/">peg display carton page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="inserts"
    heading="Inserts"
    intro="An insert restrains the product and controls what the customer sees on opening. The first job is the one that costs you money when it is missing."
  >
    <SpecTable
      caption="Insert types, their protection and their disposal route."
      columns={['Insert', 'Protection', 'Cost', 'Disposal']}
      rows={[
        ['Folded paperboard collar', 'Holds the base, stops sliding', 'Lowest', 'Paper stream with the carton'],
        ['Die-cut card platform', 'Fixes position precisely', 'Low', 'Paper stream with the carton'],
        ['Paperboard partition', 'Separates multiple items', 'Low', 'Paper stream with the carton'],
        ['Moulded pulp tray', 'Good all-round cushioning', 'Moderate', 'Paper stream, though often not kerbside'],
        ['Wrapped foam pad', 'High, conforms to shape', 'Moderate', 'Foam must be separated'],
        ['EVA or flocked plastic tray', 'Highest, most premium feel', 'Highest', 'Not paper-recyclable'],
      ]}
    />
    <p class="measure">
      Quote the insert with the carton rather than separately. An insert sourced
      on its own arrives a millimetre out, is discovered during packing, and leaves
      you choosing between forcing it and reordering.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="rigid"
    heading="Rigid constructions"
    intro="A different category: thick greyboard assembled into a permanent shape and wrapped in printed paper. It does not fold and does not ship flat."
  >
    <ul class="info-cards">
      <li>
        <h3>Magnetic book box</h3>
        <p>
          A hinged lid with concealed magnets that closes with a click. The format
          most customers associate with luxury cosmetics.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Drawer and sleeve</h3>
        <p>
          An inner tray sliding out of an outer shell, often with a ribbon pull.
          Reveals the product gradually and protects on all sides.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Two-piece lid and base</h3>
        <p>
          A separate lid lifting off a base. Sturdy, simple, and the least
          expensive of the rigid options.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Shoulder neck box</h3>
        <p>
          Lid and base separated by a visible collar, which allows a contrasting
          colour or material in the gap.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">
      Rigid boxes are viable at genuinely small quantities because setup cost is
      low relative to unit cost, which makes them practical for limited editions.
      Construction detail and material options are on the
      <a href="/rigid-lipstick-boxes/">wrapped rigid box page</a>.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="display"
    heading="Retail display and shipping structures"
    intro="Structures that hold other packs rather than a product."
  >
    <ul class="bullets">
      <li>
        <strong>Counter display units</strong> — a printed corrugated tray that
        ships flat, folds into an angled display and holds a dozen or more sticks
        at a checkout counter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shelf-ready trays</strong> — a case that opens into a display tray,
        so store staff place one unit rather than unpacking individual cartons.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Clip strips</strong> — a vertical strip carrying hanging packs,
        used to gain secondary placement outside the main fixture.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Printed mailer boxes</strong> — E-flute corrugated designed to take
        postal handling, for products shipping direct to consumers.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      A folding carton is secondary packaging, not a shipper. Posted loose it will
      arrive crushed. Either put it inside a mailer with void fill or specify a
      mailer designed for the purpose.
    </p>
  </InfoSection>

  <InfoSection
    id="choosing"
    heading="Choosing a structure quickly"
    intro="Four questions get you to the right answer in most cases."
  >
    <ol class="ordered">
      <li>
        <strong>How is it filled?</strong> Automated lines need seal end. Hand
        packing usually wants a tuck end, and an auto-bottom if volumes are high
        enough for assembly time to matter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How much does the packed carton weigh?</strong> Anything above a
        single light item points at a snap-lock or auto-bottom base.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Where is it merchandised?</strong> Peg fixtures need a tab. Shelf
        and drawer do not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Is the opening part of the product?</strong> If yes, look at
        sleeve-and-tray or rigid construction. If no, a well-finished folding
        carton does the job for far less.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </InfoSection>
</InfoLayout>
